            0.597,
            0.049,
            0.846,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Another Peek Beyond the Point"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.085,
            0.843,
            0.312
          ],
          "content": "| 1.64 × 6= 116040 × 6 = 918040 = 9.84 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 5.7 × 13.35= 5170 × 1130305= 5170 × × 1 130305 = 716000905=76.095 | 1 | 2 | 3 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_table_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.331,
            0.773,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": "? Suppose we know that $5 9 6 \\times 2 4 8 = 1 4 7 8 0 8 ,$ , can you immediately write down the product of $5 . 9 6 \\times 2 4 . 8 ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.813,
            0.32,
            0.906,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.387,
            0.846,
            0.427
          ],
          "content": "By looking at the above examples, can you frame a rule to multiply two decimals?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.432,
            0.847,
            0.49
          ],
          "content": "Multiplication of decimals is the same as the multiplication of their corresponding fractions. When multiplying fractions, we multiply the numerators and denominators, respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.49,
            0.846,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": "The product of the numerators = Product of the numbers with the decimal points removed."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.528,
            0.847,
            0.604
          ],
          "content": "Since both the denominators are of the form 1, 10, 100, 1000, … , the product of the denominators is also of the form 1 followed by zeroes. The number of zeroes in the product is the sum of the number of zeroes in each denominator."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.604,
            0.846,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": "In the product, the decimal point is placed based on the total number of zeroes in the denominator."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.643,
            0.847,
            0.7
          ],
          "content": "So, to multiply two decimals, we can multiply the two numbers obtained by removing the decimal point, and then place the decimal point appropriately as shown below."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.7,
            0.406,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": "To evaluate 5.96 × 24.8:"
